  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the most favorable outcome among those with the lowest probability. In this case, both outcomes in Lottery B have 50% probability (the minimum in that lottery), and the most favorable is 0 Euros. In Lottery A, the only outcome (-500 Euros) has 100% probability, so it is the least probable (and only) outcome. The reason favors B because 0 > -500. The individual emphasizes the 'chance to land at 0 Euros' and uses this as a justification for choosing B over the certain loss in A. While the individual does not explicitly frame their reasoning in terms of 'least probable outcomes' or compare probabilities structurally, they do focus on the positive low-probability (or equally probable) outcome in B and contrast it with the certain negative outcome in A. This aligns closely with the essence of the decision reason, even if not articulated in probabilistic terms. The core of the reason — preferring the lottery with a better outcome in its least probable (or non-certain) favorable event — matches the individual’s emphasis. Therefore, it is highly likely the individual used this type of reasoning, even if informally.",
